---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-heart-failure`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Heart Failure',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Heart failure', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'I50', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Heart Failure', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Heart Failure: LV Function, VINDICATE Trial, and Loop-Diuretic Thiamine"
  description="Heart failure and vitamin D — VINDICATE trial showed LV improvement, wider evidence, loop-diuretic thiamine concern, and dosing in HFrEF and HFpEF."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-heart-failure", label: "Heart Failure" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Heart Failure</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Heart failure patients have very high rates of vitamin D deficiency —
      often 70%+. The VINDICATE trial showed cholecalciferol supplementation
      improved left ventricular function in HFrEF patients. Correction is
      standard supportive care alongside guideline-directed HF therapy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Deficiency prevalence in HF</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Multiple observational studies find 70-90% of HF patients have 25(OH)D
      &lt; 30 ng/mL and 30-50% &lt; 20 ng/mL. Reasons include reduced sun
      exposure from mobility limitation, altered kidney function affecting
      activation, and loop-diuretic-related changes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">VINDICATE trial</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Witte 2016 randomised 229 HFrEF patients with 25(OH)D &lt; 20 ng/mL to
      4,000 IU/day vitamin D vs placebo for 1 year. Vitamin D arm showed:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>~6.7% absolute increase in LV ejection fraction.</li>
      <li>Improvement in LV end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes.</li>
      <li>No improvement in 6-minute walk distance primary endpoint.</li>
      <li>Modest quality-of-life improvement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Loop diuretics and thiamine — separate but important</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Furosemide, torsemide, and bumetanide (all loop diuretics) increase
      urinary thiamine losses. Chronic HF patients on loop diuretics develop
      thiamine deficiency at up to 33% prevalence. This is not a vitamin D
      issue but is worth screening — thiamine deficiency worsens HF, causes
      Wernicke's, and is easily correctable. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-b1" class="text-amber-700 underline">thiamine page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">HFpEF (preserved ejection fraction)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      HFpEF is increasingly recognised as a distinct syndrome — heart failure
      with normal ejection fraction, driven by diastolic dysfunction,
      hypertension, obesity, and diabetes. Vitamin D evidence in HFpEF is
      thinner than HFrEF; correcting deficiency is reasonable for general
      health but disease-specific benefit is unproven.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>All HF patients: test 25(OH)D annually.</li>
      <li>Correct deficiency: 2,000-4,000 IU/day cholecalciferol.</li>
      <li>On chronic loop diuretic: consider thiamine 100 mg/day, especially in older patients or malnourished.</li>
      <li>Guideline-directed HF therapy (ARNI/ACE-i, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i) remains the priority.</li>
      <li>Monitor calcium and creatinine — HF patients on ACE-i/ARB and MRAs can develop hyperkalaemia; vitamin D-driven calcium changes deserve attention.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Heart failure is a specialist-managed
      chronic condition; supplement decisions should fit with your cardiology
      team's plan.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-heart-failure" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
